{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"optimizer-aggregate-pushdown-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimizer aggregate pushdown oracle review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed implementation: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"37350e5b58b6006f24d9e7c68b894bac7b45ec46"},{"type":"text","value":".\nBaseline with the original optimizer: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"33a194941c8d51f8f98babb999fef2987dd6ff8b"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe review record and changeset follow the reviewed implementation. They do not\nchange the oracle or production code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"claim-and-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim and evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An outer predicate on an aggregate subquery must run after aggregation. The\noptimizer may move it below the aggregate only when that move preserves the\nresult. The original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"selectHasAggregates"},{"type":"text","value":" missed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"add(sum(v), 0)"},{"type":"text","value":" and\naggregates inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"caseWhen"},{"type":"text","value":". The optimizer moved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isUndefined(s.k)"},{"type":"text","value":" to the\nsource row's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"k"},{"type":"text","value":" field. The global aggregate omits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"k"},{"type":"text","value":" from its public row.\nThe moved predicate rejected both source rows and removed the public result."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle uses source rows "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{k:1,v:10}"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{k:2,v:20}"},{"type":"text","value":". Independent\nrecomputation gives total "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"30"},{"type":"text","value":". A materialized aggregate Collection and a\nnested aggregate under a left join must both expose the same post-filter row.\nThe direct aggregate and grouped query are nearby controls. A left join keeps\nthe aggregate row when no matching joined row exists. An earlier inner-join\nprobe returned no row even without a WHERE clause for both direct and wrapped\naggregates, so it could not isolate optimizer pushdown."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On baseline, the direct and grouped controls passed. The arithmetic wrapper,\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"caseWhen"},{"type":"text","value":" condition-only aggregate, and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"caseWhen"},{"type":"text","value":" default-only\naggregate failed the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toArray"},{"type":"text","value":" assertion. Each returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":" instead\nof the modeled row. These are assertion kills at the intended checkpoint.\nAfter the fix, all five oracle cases passed. The surrounding query directory\npassed 87 files and 3,440 tests before the two final conditional cases were\nadded. The final focused optimizer and oracle run passed 51 tests. TypeScript,\nESLint, and Prettier checks passed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-001-through-orc-011","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 through ORC-011"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 Contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQL aggregate semantics and the optimizer's documented safety rule require post-aggregate filtering. The owner checks the listed expressions and public snapshot under a left join. The coverage map names the omitted predicates, wrappers, join types, and incremental histories."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 Independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The expected sum comes from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Array.reduce"},{"type":"text","value":" over source rows. The expected outer result follows from applying the predicate after projection. Neither computation imports the optimizer's aggregate detector."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle file states the law, stateless sum model, bounded expression grammar, public Collection driver, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toArray"},{"type":"text","value":" refinement check at synchronous publication."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 Generated-history controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The file enumerates fixed cases and does not claim generated-history coverage."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 Production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createLiveQueryCollection"},{"type":"text","value":" compiles nested aggregate and outer join queries. The test compares exact public rows after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: true"},{"type":"text","value":". Direct and materialized controls show the public path reaches the aggregate and predicate."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 Checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The original optimizer is the wrong-design control. Its wrapped and conditional cases fail by value assertion at the named checkpoint. The direct and grouped controls pass on the same baseline."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 Fixed/random replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The owner has no important generated property."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The model recomputes one sum and retains no transition state."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No model-only lifecycle or state term is introduced. “Source rows,” “public row,” and “public snapshot” follow the glossary."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The fixed cases do not shrink, capture mutable traces, or run cleanup that can replace the assertion failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 Second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The materialized aggregate Collection and the nested subquery have the same public projection and predicate. The former prevents cross-boundary pushdown and catches the plausible shared-fault risk in the sum model. Each expected result has one row, so order and multiplicity do not need normalization."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bounded repair covers the contract × fixed source rows × nested left-join\npath × first public snapshot cells above. It does not prove all optimizer\nrewrites safe. The coverage map owns the remaining cells and the separate\nglobal-aggregate inner-join observation."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"optimizer-aggregate-pushdown-oracle-review","text":"Optimizer aggregate pushdown oracle review","level":1},{"id":"claim-and-evidence","text":"Claim and evidence","level":2},{"id":"orc-001-through-orc-011","text":"ORC-001 through ORC-011","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Optimizer aggregate pushdown oracle review\n\nReviewed implementation: `37350e5b58b6006f24d9e7c68b894bac7b45ec46`.\nBaseline with the original optimizer: `33a194941c8d51f8f98babb999fef2987dd6ff8b`.\nThe review record and changeset follow the reviewed implementation. They do not\nchange the oracle or production code.\n\n## Claim and evidence\n\nAn outer predicate on an aggregate subquery must run after aggregation. The\noptimizer may move it below the aggregate only when that move preserves the\nresult. The original `selectHasAggregates` missed `add(sum(v), 0)` and\naggregates inside `caseWhen`. The optimizer moved `isUndefined(s.k)` to the\nsource row's `k` field. The global aggregate omits `k` from its public row.\nThe moved predicate rejected both source rows and removed the public result.\n\nThe oracle uses source rows `{k:1,v:10}` and `{k:2,v:20}`. Independent\nrecomputation gives total `30`. A materialized aggregate Collection and a\nnested aggregate under a left join must both expose the same post-filter row.\nThe direct aggregate and grouped query are nearby controls. A left join keeps\nthe aggregate row when no matching joined row exists. An earlier inner-join\nprobe returned no row even without a WHERE clause for both direct and wrapped\naggregates, so it could not isolate optimizer pushdown.\n\nOn baseline, the direct and grouped controls passed. The arithmetic wrapper,\nthe `caseWhen` condition-only aggregate, and the `caseWhen` default-only\naggregate failed the public `toArray` assertion. Each returned `[]` instead\nof the modeled row. These are assertion kills at the intended checkpoint.\nAfter the fix, all five oracle cases passed. The surrounding query directory\npassed 87 files and 3,440 tests before the two final conditional cases were\nadded. The final focused optimizer and oracle run passed 51 tests. TypeScript,\nESLint, and Prettier checks passed.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-011\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | SQL aggregate semantics and the optimizer's documented safety rule require post-aggregate filtering. The owner checks the listed expressions and public snapshot under a left join. The coverage map names the omitted predicates, wrappers, join types, and incremental histories. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | The expected sum comes from `Array.reduce` over source rows. The expected outer result follows from applying the predicate after projection. Neither computation imports the optimizer's aggregate detector. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | The oracle file states the law, stateless sum model, bounded expression grammar, public Collection driver, and `toArray` refinement check at synchronous publication. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Not applicable. The file enumerates fixed cases and does not claim generated-history coverage. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | `createLiveQueryCollection` compiles nested aggregate and outer join queries. The test compares exact public rows after `startSync: true`. Direct and materialized controls show the public path reaches the aggregate and predicate. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | The original optimizer is the wrong-design control. Its wrapped and conditional cases fail by value assertion at the named checkpoint. The direct and grouped controls pass on the same baseline. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Not applicable. The owner has no important generated property. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. The model recomputes one sum and retains no transition state. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | No model-only lifecycle or state term is introduced. “Source rows,” “public row,” and “public snapshot” follow the glossary. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Not applicable. The fixed cases do not shrink, capture mutable traces, or run cleanup that can replace the assertion failure. |\n| ORC-011 Second formulation | The materialized aggregate Collection and the nested subquery have the same public projection and predicate. The former prevents cross-boundary pushdown and catches the plausible shared-fault risk in the sum model. Each expected result has one row, so order and multiplicity do not need normalization. |\n\nThe bounded repair covers the contract × fixed source rows × nested left-join\npath × first public snapshot cells above. It does not prove all optimizer\nrewrites safe. The coverage map owns the remaining cells and the separate\nglobal-aggregate inner-join observation.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Optimizer aggregate pushdown oracle review\n\nReviewed implementation: `37350e5b58b6006f24d9e7c68b894bac7b45ec46`.\nBaseline with the original optimizer: `33a194941c8d51f8f98babb999fef2987dd6ff8b`.\nThe review record and changeset follow the reviewed implementation. They do not\nchange the oracle or production code.\n\n## Claim and evidence\n\nAn outer predicate on an aggregate subquery must run after aggregation. The\noptimizer may move it below the aggregate only when that move preserves the\nresult. The original `selectHasAggregates` missed `add(sum(v), 0)` and\naggregates inside `caseWhen`. The optimizer moved `isUndefined(s.k)` to the\nsource row's `k` field. The global aggregate omits `k` from its public row.\nThe moved predicate rejected both source rows and removed the public result.\n\nThe oracle uses source rows `{k:1,v:10}` and `{k:2,v:20}`. Independent\nrecomputation gives total `30`. A materialized aggregate Collection and a\nnested aggregate under a left join must both expose the same post-filter row.\nThe direct aggregate and grouped query are nearby controls. A left join keeps\nthe aggregate row when no matching joined row exists. An earlier inner-join\nprobe returned no row even without a WHERE clause for both direct and wrapped\naggregates, so it could not isolate optimizer pushdown.\n\nOn baseline, the direct and grouped controls passed. The arithmetic wrapper,\nthe `caseWhen` condition-only aggregate, and the `caseWhen` default-only\naggregate failed the public `toArray` assertion. Each returned `[]` instead\nof the modeled row. These are assertion kills at the intended checkpoint.\nAfter the fix, all five oracle cases passed. The surrounding query directory\npassed 87 files and 3,440 tests before the two final conditional cases were\nadded. The final focused optimizer and oracle run passed 51 tests. TypeScript,\nESLint, and Prettier checks passed.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-011\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | SQL aggregate semantics and the optimizer's documented safety rule require post-aggregate filtering. The owner checks the listed expressions and public snapshot under a left join. The coverage map names the omitted predicates, wrappers, join types, and incremental histories. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | The expected sum comes from `Array.reduce` over source rows. The expected outer result follows from applying the predicate after projection. Neither computation imports the optimizer's aggregate detector. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | The oracle file states the law, stateless sum model, bounded expression grammar, public Collection driver, and `toArray` refinement check at synchronous publication. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Not applicable. The file enumerates fixed cases and does not claim generated-history coverage. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | `createLiveQueryCollection` compiles nested aggregate and outer join queries. The test compares exact public rows after `startSync: true`. Direct and materialized controls show the public path reaches the aggregate and predicate. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | The original optimizer is the wrong-design control. Its wrapped and conditional cases fail by value assertion at the named checkpoint. The direct and grouped controls pass on the same baseline. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Not applicable. The owner has no important generated property. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. The model recomputes one sum and retains no transition state. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | No model-only lifecycle or state term is introduced. “Source rows,” “public row,” and “public snapshot” follow the glossary. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Not applicable. The fixed cases do not shrink, capture mutable traces, or run cleanup that can replace the assertion failure. |\n| ORC-011 Second formulation | The materialized aggregate Collection and the nested subquery have the same public projection and predicate. The former prevents cross-boundary pushdown and catches the plausible shared-fault risk in the sum model. Each expected result has one row, so order and multiplicity do not need normalization. |\n\nThe bounded repair covers the contract × fixed source rows × nested left-join\npath × first public snapshot cells above. It does not prove all optimizer\nrewrites safe. The coverage map owns the remaining cells and the separate\nglobal-aggregate inner-join observation.\n"}
